// A BLANKET BOUNDED BY A PACKAGE-LOCAL TRAIT MUST NOT BE CLONED FOR TYPES THAT
// ONLY IMPLEMENT THE STDLIB HOMONYM.
//
// `impl<T: Hash> Marker for T` is bounded by THIS package's `Hash`, which only
// `S` implements. Nothing here calls `Marker` on an `i32`. The program must
// compile and run.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS PINS IS COMPILABILITY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. Before the
// fix this file did not produce a wrong answer — it did not produce a program
// at all:
//     error: 'func.call' op 'i32__tag' does not reference a valid function
//     rc 1
// Mono's eager blanket pass built its candidate list by comparing the impl's
// trait SPELLING with the blanket's bound SPELLING, so every type implementing
// the STDLIB `Hash` (i32, i64, …) was collected as a candidate, the blanket
// body was cloned for each, and the clone called `tag()` — a method of THIS
// package's `Hash` that an `i32` does not have. Both sides now compare the
// always-qualified trait IDENTITY.
//
// ⚠ THE ASSERTION IS NOT DECORATION. `s.mark()` must return 91 — the value that
// only reaches it through THIS package's `Hash::tag`. A fix that made the
// program compile by dropping the local trait's facts would either fail to
// resolve `mark` or route it elsewhere, and the `return 1` arm catches that.
// The blanket must still work; it must merely stop working for the wrong types.
//
// ⚠ NOT PINNED: the verifier line above. It is the defect. A pass fixture that
// somehow encoded it would be asserting one.
//
// Single TU, no archive: the stdlib is collected first and owns the bare `Hash`
// registry slot, so this package's `Hash` is the one under a qualified key.
// That is stated because it decides WHICH of the two is the homonym here, and
// the mirror (an archive's trait owning the bare slot) is a different question
// — see tests/logos/fail/trait_ident_cross_archive_bound_refused.logos.
package trait_blanket_homonym_no_overinstantiation;

pub trait Hash { fn tag(&self) -> i64; }
pub trait Marker { fn mark(&self) -> i64; }

pub struct S { pub a: i32 }
impl Hash for S { fn tag(&self) -> i64 { return 91i64; } }

// The blanket. Its body calls the BOUND's method, which is what turns a wrong
// candidate list into an unresolvable call rather than a silent wrong answer.
impl<T: Hash> Marker for T { fn mark(&self) -> i64 { return self.tag(); } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let s: S = S { a: 1i32 };
    if s.mark() != 91i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
